The tempting trial $\mathbf B\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}$ is a scalar and contains a velocity, but it gives the wrong equation of motion. The correct term uses the vector potential: $L_{\mathrm{mag}}
  =
  \frac{e}{c}\mathbf A(\mathbf x)\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}
  =
  \frac{e}{c}A_i(\mathbf x)\dot x_i.$ Thus the full time-independent electromagnetic Lagrangian is $\boxed{
  L
  =
  \frac{m}{2}\dot{\mathbf x}^{\,2}
  +\frac{e}{c}\mathbf A(\mathbf x)\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}
  -eV(\mathbf x)
  }.$ Lecture 10 will apply the Euler–Lagrange equations and show explicitly that this expression produces the Lorentz force.

